WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:08.860
For half of the planet, mosquitoes are more
than just a nuisance.

00:00:09.140 --> 00:00:14.100
Their bites transmit parasites and viruses
that kill millions of people each year.

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:18.320
One of them, dengue virus, 
causes dengue fever.

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:20.760
It's often called breakbone fever

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:24.380
because the patients feel like the bones 
in their bodies are being broken.

00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:31.040
If you get sick with dengue, you can have
mild symptoms through to very extreme symptoms,

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:33.740
where you're going to have internal bleeding,

00:00:33.740 --> 00:00:35.460
shock and can die.

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:36.920
So it’s a big spectrum

00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:39.770
but if you speak to anybody that's had 
a bad dose of dengue,

00:00:39.770 --> 00:00:43.600
they'll tell you it's one of the worst things
that happened to them in their life.

00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:49.080
Dengue is considered the world’s fastest
spreading tropical disease.

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:53.180
Its cases have risen thirtyfold in the last 50 years.

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:56.140
Dengue is like everywhere in the tropics.

00:00:56.140 --> 00:01:01.080
Potentially 40% of the world's population
is at risk this year of getting dengue.

00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:02.560
That's a big number.

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:05.000
We're talking about billions of people.

00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:08.260
And with the world’s temperatures rising,

00:01:08.260 --> 00:01:14.340
dengue can spread out of the tropics potentially
reaching a further two billion people by 2080.

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:19.460
In Indonesia the total number of dengue cases
is second in the world.

00:01:19.580 --> 00:01:23.340
It has been proven very difficult to sort of

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:27.040
get rid of all the mosquitoes causing dengue.

00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:31.220
In the laboratories chemicals are discovered
for killing mosquitoes.

00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:35.980
We’ve tried very hard but still, 
the mosquitoes are around us.

00:01:36.520 --> 00:01:40.120
So it needs something else to combat dengue.

00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:52.200
Eight years ago Scott O’Neill founded The
World Mosquito Program,

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:56.640
a non-profit initiative running trials in
12 countries around the world,

00:01:56.640 --> 00:01:58.560
one of them here in Indonesia.

00:01:58.840 --> 00:02:01.360
Their sole aim is to eradicate dengue.

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:04.640
In particular one mosquito, Aedes aegypti,

00:02:04.640 --> 00:02:08.840
it is responsible for transmitting between
people a number of diseases.

00:02:08.840 --> 00:02:11.760
Some of them obscure, some of them quite famous.

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:17.000
So diseases like yellow fever, dengue, chikungunya,
and most recently, Zika.

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:21.120
And the reason that this particular mosquito,
Aedes aegypti, is so good

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:23.560
at being able to cause these explosive epidemics

00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:26.439
is because it's like the cockroach of the
mosquito world.

00:02:26.440 --> 00:02:29.080
Just like cockroaches, 
it likes to live with people.

00:02:29.080 --> 00:02:33.000
It doesn't live out in the forest in harmony
with nature or whatever,

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:36.520
it lives in people's houses, in concrete jungles, in cities,

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:39.840
and it bites people, maybe two or three times a day.

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:41.960
And so often whole families get sick with dengue,

00:02:41.960 --> 00:02:47.120
because that one mosquito bit everybody in
that house within a period of a couple of days.

00:02:49.640 --> 00:02:53.840
As soon as people discovered 
that mosquitoes transmit disease,

00:02:53.859 --> 00:02:56.279
the immediate response is let's kill mosquitoes.

00:02:56.279 --> 00:02:59.430
The thing is all those diseases are still
pretty well here.

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:03.600
And the reason is that 
mosquitoes are really tough to kill

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:07.560
and if you realize where they breed, 
you know just in a tiny bit of water,

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:13.120
you realize that there are millions and millions
of places where these mosquitoes are breeding

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:15.920
and a thought of killing 
every single one of them

00:03:16.600 --> 00:03:19.080
just seems impossible.

00:03:20.440 --> 00:03:24.320
Most researchers have focused on genetically
modifying mosquitoes,

00:03:24.320 --> 00:03:27.040
or trying to eradicate the species entirely.

00:03:27.320 --> 00:03:32.520
Instead, O’Neill’s team is infecting mosquitoes
with bacteria - not to kill them,

00:03:32.520 --> 00:03:34.200
but to inoculate them.

00:03:35.560 --> 00:03:38.760
It’s a naturally occurring bacterium called Wolbachia.

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:41.480
That bacterium spreads into the mosquito population,

00:03:41.480 --> 00:03:46.260
and once the mosquitoes have it, they're
unable to transmit the virus between people.

00:03:47.080 --> 00:03:51.720
You know, I’ve had this obsession for a long time 
of working on Wolbachia.

00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:57.520
It occurs naturally in around 60 to 70% of
all insect species all around the world so

00:03:57.880 --> 00:04:03.080
wherever you live, if you were to go outside,
grab some insects out of the nearest bush

00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:06.269
you’d likely find, more often than not,
that those insects

00:04:06.280 --> 00:04:09.000
naturally have this bacteria called Wolbachia.

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:14.160
The mosquito though, that transmits all these
viruses to people, doesn't have it.

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:19.000
When we were able to put the Wolbachia 
into the mosquito

00:04:19.360 --> 00:04:22.120
and then we fed those mosquitoes in a laboratory virus,

00:04:22.120 --> 00:04:25.880
we found that just Wolbachia by itself, 
without any fancy tricks,

00:04:25.880 --> 00:04:28.480
would stop the transmission of the viruses.

00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:32.040
Wolbachia was stopping, not just dengue, but
yellow fever,

00:04:32.040 --> 00:04:35.680
chikungunya, Zika, Mayaro, a whole range of viruses

00:04:35.680 --> 00:04:39.760
and so it was a major discovery.

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:42.120
But introducing Wolbachia bacteria

00:04:42.120 --> 00:04:46.210
to disease carrying mosquitoes like the Aedes
aegypti isn’t easy.

00:04:46.210 --> 00:04:51.920
In fact, the only way to do it is to inject
freshly laid mosquito eggs with the bacterium.

00:04:51.960 --> 00:04:53.919
This process takes time.

00:04:53.919 --> 00:04:58.810
O’Neill’s team has spent more than five years
building their colony of a few thousand mosquitoes.

00:04:58.810 --> 00:05:00.720
But that was the hard part.

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:03.000
Now, nature takes over.

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:08.560
This bacterium transmits itself vertically
from a mother to her offspring,

00:05:08.590 --> 00:05:10.520
so it gets carried in the egg.

00:05:10.520 --> 00:05:14.379
So what Wolbachia does is that if it's in
a female it will be transmitted

00:05:14.379 --> 00:05:19.000
and so that female can mate with either males
that have Wolbachia, or males that don't

00:05:19.080 --> 00:05:22.040
and she'll produce eggs, and those eggs 
will all have Wolbachia.

00:05:22.040 --> 00:05:23.720
The trick though is the other way.

00:05:23.729 --> 00:05:28.389
If the male has Wolbachia, but the female doesn't
have Wolbachia,

00:05:28.389 --> 00:05:30.590
then she will lay eggs and all her eggs will die.

00:05:30.600 --> 00:05:35.680
The end result of that is that only females
that have Wolbachia are able to reproduce.

00:05:35.680 --> 00:05:40.430
And so Wolbachia then spreads into the insect population without having to be infectious,

00:05:40.440 --> 00:05:43.960
without having to jump from one individual to another.

00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:50.720
In Yogyakarta, whenever you go to the community
and you ask,

00:05:50.720 --> 00:05:53.560
”Do you know anyone that had dengue before?”

00:05:54.040 --> 00:05:58.560
my guess is that everyone will answer "Yes."

00:05:58.560 --> 00:06:05.640
Because it's so common, and it's still created
a panic within the community.

00:06:07.160 --> 00:06:12.189
People often, who live in transmission areas,
live with a lot of fear about dengue.

00:06:12.200 --> 00:06:16.600
Actually it's a family of viruses. 
It can be grouped into four groups.

00:06:16.680 --> 00:06:20.800
And unimaginatively, they're called dengue 1, 2, 3, and 4.

00:06:20.800 --> 00:06:25.400
When you get infected, say, with dengue 1,
your body makes antibodies against dengue 1,

00:06:25.400 --> 00:06:31.039
and so it's much harder for you to then acquire
dengue 1 again, the second time.

00:06:31.039 --> 00:06:34.270
But those antibodies don't protect you for
dengue 2, 3, or 4.

00:06:34.270 --> 00:06:39.229
But not only that, those antibodies will
make it easier for dengue 2, 3, and 4

00:06:39.240 --> 00:06:44.800
to get into your body and cause disease and
actually create potentially more severe disease.

00:06:44.800 --> 00:06:48.000
And so it's possible that 
you could get dengue four times.

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:53.120
And each time you get it, you could potentially
become more sick and at greater risk of dying.

00:06:56.400 --> 00:07:00.000
Yogyakarta is where the group is conducting
a major study.

00:07:00.050 --> 00:07:06.020
They’re collecting data on how successful
Wolbachia is in stopping dengue transmissions.

00:07:06.020 --> 00:07:09.870
The first batch of mosquitoes 
was released here in 2014.

00:07:09.870 --> 00:07:15.280
We worked with the community far before we
released the mosquitoes

00:07:15.280 --> 00:07:19.360
to make sure that they understand that what
we are targeting is the virus,

00:07:19.360 --> 00:07:21.760
not just the mosquitoes.

00:07:27.120 --> 00:07:32.240
This project would not have been possible
without the community understanding.

00:07:32.240 --> 00:07:35.819
Because the idea was like a controversy.

00:07:35.819 --> 00:07:37.220
Let's think about it for a minute.

00:07:37.220 --> 00:07:41.040
We're going to come in, a bunch of scientists

00:07:41.040 --> 00:07:44.480
and we're going to release mosquitoes 
into the community.

00:07:45.280 --> 00:07:47.009
And these mosquitoes are going to bite you.

00:07:47.009 --> 00:07:51.180
And all you've heard for the last 50 years
of your life is that you have to kill mosquitoes,

00:07:51.180 --> 00:07:52.180
because they're dangerous.

00:07:52.180 --> 00:07:55.040
You would imagine the community 
would be very cautious.

00:07:55.360 --> 00:07:58.560
I'd be cautious or I’d wanna know a lot
about what’s going on.

00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:03.280
And I think community is like that, and we understand
that, and we've really tried to address that.

00:08:03.280 --> 00:08:07.200
And by doing that, we've had virtually no opposition.

00:08:07.920 --> 00:08:11.600
Communities are a huge part of the project
and the team spends time

00:08:11.639 --> 00:08:14.180
explaining the science behind their intervention.

00:08:14.180 --> 00:08:18.160
But they’re also involving them 
in the mosquito releases.

00:08:18.160 --> 00:08:23.680
People take in the buckets with mosquito eggs
and take care of them until the eggs hatch.

00:08:25.360 --> 00:08:29.280
In this space mosquitoes are being grown 
that contain Wolbachia.

00:08:29.360 --> 00:08:33.680
So that we can collect eggs off them and then those eggs will be distributed into the community.

00:08:33.680 --> 00:08:37.120
In Yogyakarta we're using human volunteers for feeding.

00:08:38.240 --> 00:08:44.080
Some of the researchers have been bitten probably more than a million times by mosquitoes with Wolbachia.

00:08:44.080 --> 00:08:46.320
All for the sake of disease prevention.

00:08:47.920 --> 00:08:51.340
The team keeps monitoring how many mosquitoes
in the wild have Wolbachia,

00:08:51.360 --> 00:08:55.360
and continues to release them until they reach
a certain threshold.

00:08:56.240 --> 00:09:00.460
Once this is done, the bacteria sustain themselves
in the mosquito population

00:09:00.480 --> 00:09:02.960
and the method doesn’t have to be repeated.

00:09:03.840 --> 00:09:07.640
Current data shows around 70% 
reduction in dengue cases

00:09:07.640 --> 00:09:10.700
in the areas where Wolbachia mosquitoes 
were released.

00:09:10.700 --> 00:09:15.490
But we think it's a big underestimate, because
if you spend a little bit of time in Yogyakarta,

00:09:15.490 --> 00:09:17.790
you'll notice that everybody's on motorbikes
traveling everywhere.

00:09:17.790 --> 00:09:20.840
And so it's possible that they've gone out,

00:09:20.900 --> 00:09:25.280
been bitten by a mosquito and then come back in and then get counted as in the intervention area.

00:09:25.980 --> 00:09:30.420
And it looks like, you know, everything that we've seen in the laboratory, our mathematical modeling,

00:09:30.420 --> 00:09:34.140
everything is coming together to suggest that
we're gonna have a big impact on disease.

00:09:37.780 --> 00:09:40.960
By the end of this year the team’s Wolbachia
method will cover

00:09:40.960 --> 00:09:43.380
about three million people around the world.

00:09:43.880 --> 00:09:47.780
But they want to reach at least 100 million by 2023.

00:09:48.760 --> 00:09:52.660
The ambition for our program is big, but the
problem is huge.

00:09:52.660 --> 00:09:54.540
Eliminating diseases is very challenging.

00:09:54.580 --> 00:09:58.960
I think if you look at polio today, you can bring
polio down to very low levels.

00:09:58.960 --> 00:10:03.820
But to do the final elimination, so there
is no more cases, very challenging.

00:10:03.820 --> 00:10:05.480
Smallpox is an example,

00:10:05.980 --> 00:10:08.080
there aren't too many others out there.

00:10:13.700 --> 00:10:18.620
Spreading Wolbachia mosquitoes around half
of the planet is a monumental task.

00:10:18.620 --> 00:10:23.660
It would require a huge injection of funding
and a coordinated effort of governments.

00:10:26.240 --> 00:10:32.640
But the three billion people at risk of such dangerous
viruses motivates the team to keep fighting.

00:10:33.400 --> 00:10:36.760
There's an obsessive nature to the work that we do.

00:10:36.900 --> 00:10:42.060
And I think that sits behind a lot of science,
to have an idea and then to really hold onto it

00:10:42.160 --> 00:10:44.840
and then work and work and work and work,
until we’re successful.

00:10:45.060 --> 00:10:49.160
And so my hope would be that we could eliminate
dengue at some point.

